        "problem": "Find ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t$ \ngiven that \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=4\\,i+2\\,k$ \nand \n$c=i+j-k.$",
        "worked_solution": "The cross-product rule states that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t=c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nApplying the cross-product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}c\\times f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =c\\times {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(i+j-k)\\times (4\\,i+2\\,k) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =(2-0)i-(2+4)j+(0-4)k \\\\  & =2i-6j-4k.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
